{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790340000000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r1.glm-5.3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:04:09 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:04:09 +07\n     3\t\n     4\t# dao.stream.serve — review of Fable's P2P-invariant revision (GLM-5.3, round 1)\n     5\t\n     6\tVerdict up front: **the dissolution is sound — relay and rendezvous really are derivable as ordinary peers serving ordinary streams, and \"nothing privileged remains\" is true at the protocol level. The mechanism has two real defects (one decisive: the punch socket rule) and one privacy regression.** Of the revised positions: I AGREE on Q2 and Q3 (new evidence, not convergence), AGREE on Q4 with one addition; the earlier consensus should not stand for Q2/Q3 as written. Q1, Q5, Q6, Q7 are genuinely unaffected. I verified every section-5 citation, including `rpc.cljc` `server-state`/`serve-once!` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:100,106` — per-session roles, no change needed, as Fable says), `dao.stream.discovery.md:119-125`, and `dao.jing.dht.md:163-167`.\n     7\t\n     8\t## 1. The mechanism (section 3)\n     9\t\n    10\t**Sound and derivable.** I traced every element to the contract: a writer-only served stream (surface restriction, findings.md:91-99), a reader-only single-writer served stream, appends through ordinary sessions (concurrent writers linearize by the source's sequence, findings.md:278), reads cursor-keyed and idempotent so UDP loss/reorder at the meeting layer is absorbed by the existing design (findings.md:109-117) — Fable's claim that an evicting inbox yields inner `gap`s that the inner retry absorbs checks out, because inner requests/responses are re-derivable by idempotence. The revised frame set (open/opened/disclaim/close/request/response/ping/pong) hides no node kind, and \"every peer runs both steps\" plus the owner's rulings (conventions are not privileges; dialer/acceptor is an establishment fact) close the invariant. Criterion 5's inversion (a dial-only peer serving through an inbox on a dial-only third peer, whose table holds \"two ring buffers and no other state about the session\") is correct. The double framing is stated honestly. **\"Nothing privileged remains\" is true as claimed** — the residual facts (M must be reachable; M chose the convention) are, per the owner's ruling (1), not privileges.\n    11\t\n    12\t**Defect 1 — decisive: the punch must come from the same socket that produced the published reflexive address.** Revised §7.4 says each party \"`attach!`es a UDP channel\" to the other's reflexive address and pings. But `attach!` binds a **fresh local socket** (findings.md:192), and a NAT mapping exists per internal endpoint — a new local port means a new external mapping under **every** NAT type, not just symmetric ones. So the address M published (the mapping of the socket that talked to M) is stale on arrival; the ping that reaches the peer comes from a mapping it was never told, and punching as drafted succeeds only where the *receiving* side's filtering is permissive (full-cone). Classic STUN/ICE works for all cone NATs precisely because checks are sent from the same socket whose mapping was observed. Two required edits: (a) §7.4 — punch from the channel used to reach M, i.e. through the **same socket** whose mapping `:meet/seen` reported; (b) §6.3 — the udp seam must allow sending to an explicit destination on an existing socket, the direct channel being the new attachment that socket acquires when the peer's ping arrives. And criterion 6's NAT simulator must be **restricted-cone**; a full-cone simulator would pass a degraded implementation.\n    13\t\n    14\t**Defect 2 — the reflexive correlation is glossed.** §7.3 says `:meet/seen` is \"from `:udp/attachment` on the request,\" but the attachment identity lives on the **channel's traffic medium**, and the appended request value carries no address; the serve layer \"interprets operations but never values\" (findings.md:273), so nothing stamps it automatically. The meeting interpreter must correlate the appended request with the channel event **by session identity** (both media carry it; sessions are `[opener-peer-id n]`). One sentence fixes it (wording in §4 below).\n    15\t\n    16\t**Regression Fable's table omits — privacy.** The original `:serve/introduce` was addressed to the two peers (findings.md:232); the revision publishes every peer's reflexive address (`:meet/seen`) as facts on a shared, publicly readable announcement stream — a home IP:port exposure to every reader of M's stream. Minimum fix: state it in the honest-costs line; better fix at zero mechanism cost: M delivers the other party's reflexive via each peer's own inbox (`:meet/caller <id> :meet/reflexive {…}` appended to the called peer's `in`), so the shared stream carries only peer ids.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Two smaller requirements.** (i) `:meet/here` is an append — non-idempotent; a lost response (`:serve/append-unknown`, findings.md:115) followed by a re-request on a fresh session can double the inbox. The convention must state the re-read discipline: on unknown, re-read announcements from a cursor minted **before** the append (the contract's mint-before-invoke rule, `dao.stream.md:496-499`) before re-appending. (ii) The announcement stream's retention must exceed intended reader lag (the same sizing relation as `dao.lease.md:259`), or a lagging peer gaps over its own inbox fact and reachability discovery silently degrades.\n    19\t\n    20\t## 2. Q2, Q3, Q4\n    21\t\n    22\t**Q2 — AGREE with the revision; the consensus position should not stand.** This is an evidence-mover, not a concession. My deferral rested on \"network chatter is a policy knob\": in the original design a dialed channel **pushes** inbound frames into a local medium, so a parked reader polls host-locally and only its own demands cost wire. The revision removes that for NAT'd peers: a peer reachable through M has no pushed channel — its inbound path is a proxy over a serve session, so **reachability itself is a standing poll of the inbox proxy**, and inbound-connection latency is bounded by poll cadence. Held reads are the only mechanism in the protocol that lets a server answer later without a new request, turning per-retry traffic into per-budget traffic and restoring push-like delivery over a poll protocol. Two precisions: hold state is per-session and dies with the session (the proxy's demand machinery re-issues — state this, per findings.md:131's session scoping); and the timing should be definite, matching Fable's own second horn — held reads land **with the meeting-convention milestone** (criteria 5-6), not necessarily with serve-core loopback/direct (criteria 1-4, 7, 9 need none of it).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Q3 — AGREE with the revision.** \"Composition-assigned\" (my phrase in the mob, reading findings.md:284) presupposed an assigner; with no relay there is none, and each peer's own composition assigning its id *is* self-minting — the revision disambiguates correctly rather than overturning. Self-minted 128-bit-random minimum makes collisions negligible; hash-of-public-key as the intended format aligns with `dao.stream.discovery.md:70-80` mechanism 1 and with the mob's own r2/r3 trajectory (verification is the authenticated channel's registration handshake, additive). Duplicate `:meet/here` as an ordinary fact handled by M's convention matches the mob's duplicate-registration rule, relocated to where an authority no longer exists. One caveat that must ride along: until the additive verification lands, a self-minted id is still an **unauthenticated name** (`dao.stream.md:1087-1092`) — the intended format must not be read as delivering authentication, and the honest-costs line should say so.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Q4 — AGREE; the consensus substance survives restated.** No relay to admit, but the consensus's content maps exactly: admission is M's interpreter rule over its own request medium (the contract gates nothing, `dao.stream.md:285-292`), caps and lifecycle become \"M bounds the number and retention of inboxes it serves,\" postage remains the intended answer for a public meeting stream (`dao.stream.discovery.md:158-162`, design unspecified at `:231-233`). Add the announcement-retention sizing sentence (item 1(ii) above) to the same policy family.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 3. Q1, Q5, Q6, Q7\n    29\t\n    30\t**Q1 — unaffected, confirmed.** Append semantics, the event-medium outcome, and the UCF `:put` amendment are untouched; a source outcome displaced through a relayed channel is still \"the observed source outcome,\" just observable later.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Q5 — unaffected, confirmed — and reinforced.** The proxy's cached-read semantics are identical, and the revision multiplies the case the amended definitions were written for: an inbox-channel reader is a proxy whose channel legs are themselves proxies, i.e. deferred observation all the way down. The consensus amendment text (orchestrator file, lines 24-36) needs no change.\n    33\t\n    34\t**Q6 — unaffected, with one note.** Exporter and resumer are per-migration roles, covered by the owner's convention clause. The note: the UCF migration acceptance's \"adequate lifetime\" now spans **two** reclamation layers — the exporter's served entry (lease-governed) *and* M's idle-inbox policy; a resumer can lose reachability because M reclaimed, not because a lease lapsed, and it surfaces as a failed candidate / `:serve/disclaim`, not a lease fact. One sentence in §9 or criterion 8, so the acceptance test fixes M's policy for the duration.\n    35\t\n    36\t**Q7 — unaffected, strengthened as Fable says.** The retitle of §5, deletion of the relay/rendezvous sections, and re-describing the copy composition as a broadcast convention are all in the consensus's direction.\n    37\t\n    38\t## 4. Edits to sections 5 and 6 (exact wording)\n    39\t\n    40\t1. **§7.4, replace the first sentence:** \"After a call is announced, each party punches **through the same UDP channel it used to reach M** — the socket whose mapping `:meet/seen` reported — sending `:serve/ping` to the other's reflexive address on the schedule its driver's `now` allows. The udp channel seam therefore permits sending to an explicit destination on an existing socket, and the direct channel is the attachment that socket acquires when the other's ping arrives. **A fresh `attach!` mints a fresh socket and therefore a fresh NAT mapping under every NAT type; punching from a new socket succeeds only against permissive (full-cone) filtering and is not this design's punch.** No pong within the punch budget means the next candidate…\" — and in §13 criterion 6, after \"NAT simulator,\" insert \"(**restricted-cone**, so the same-socket rule is load-bearing)\".\n    41\t2. **§7.3, add after the `:meet/seen` fact:** \"The reflexive address of a request is the channel attachment identity its session arrived on; the meeting interpreter correlates the appended request with that attachment by session identity, which the channel's medium and the request both carry, and publishes the pair. A requester whose `:meet/here` ends `append-unknown` re-reads the announcement stream from a cursor minted before the append before re-appending, so an inbox is not doubled. The announcement stream's retention exceeds the lag of the readers M intends to serve.\"\n    42\t3. **§7.3 honest costs, add:** \"A relayed value is framed twice, a reachable peer holds one demand open against its inbox, and `:meet/seen` facts on a shared announcement stream expose reflexive addresses to every reader — a convention may instead deliver the caller's reflexive through the called peer's own inbox — and until channel authentication exists, a peer id is an unauthenticated name (`dao.stream.md`, *Recorded, not proposed*).\"\n    43\t4. **§7.1, third candidate kind, one-word consistency fix** (the inventory row says punching is \"a resolution rule… not a candidate kind\"): call it \"a candidate whose resolution is the meeting procedure of §7.3,\" so the candidate list and the inventory agree.\n    44\t5. **Section 5 table, add two rows:** (high) original design §7.4 `:serve/introduce` was addressed to the two peers (findings.md:232) while the revision publishes `:meet/seen` to all readers — the privacy regression above; (medium) §7.4 punch socket — the same-socket rule, without which criterion 6 is satisfiable by a degraded implementation.\n    45\t\n    46\tI hold these on evidence, not to converge: the mechanism is the right shape, the invariant reading is faithful to the owner's rulings, and the defects above are fixable by the wording given without reopening any settled question.\n    47\t\n    48\tCONSENSUS (my positions this round): mechanism sound with the five edits; Q2 revised-include (meeting-convention milestone), Q3 self-minted, Q4 restated — consensus Q2/Q3 superseded, Q4 substance survives; Q1/Q5/Q6/Q7 unaffected.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790340000000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r1.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790340000000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r1.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:05:29 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:05:29 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\t## Mechanism\n     5\t\n     6\t**The meeting convention is permitted by the owner’s rulings, but the revised NAT path is not yet mechanically derived from DaoStream alone.** Every peer can run the same serve protocol, and a reachable peer may choose to host meeting streams. That removes a privileged *protocol node kind*. The meeting peer still performs routing, address observation, allocation, and fanout as a composition with costs and policy that must be specified. [revision:42](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:42), [revision:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55)\n     7\t\n     8\tFour gaps prevent the claimed mechanical result:\n     9\t\n    10\t- **Surfaces and provenance.** The serving rule exposes the supplied handle’s declared surface. The meeting interpreter needs private read access to requests and private write access to announcements, while visitors get the opposite surfaces; that requires explicit restricted handles. More seriously, a `:meet/here` value in the request medium does not contain the UDP attachment identity that §3 says M republishes as `:meet/seen`. DaoStream’s attachment correlation is delivered to the composition, not automatically inserted into appended values. [original design:91](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:91), [revision:46](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:46), [revision:52](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:52), [dao.stream.md:272](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:272)\n    11\t- **UDP punching.** The observed reflexive address must belong to the *same bound UDP socket* used for probes. The original UDP `attach!` binds an ephemeral socket; a fresh attachment to the other peer can use another port. A matching nonce-bearing pong, rather than the first inbound datagram, must establish bidirectional reachability. [original design:192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:192), [revision:160](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:160)\n    12\t- **Nested loss and size.** Two proxied streams can carry inner serve frames, but an outer inbox `gap` is loss of *channel frames*, not a `gap` in the final served stream. The inner driver needs a bounded recovery rule for lost or reordered control frames and append-unknown across sessions. Double framing also reduces the UDP payload budget; the existing design refuses an oversized single value rather than making every stream value transferable. [revision:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55), [original design:113](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113), [original design:121](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:121), [original design:193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:193)\n    13\t- **Fairness.** One accepted `:meet/here` can allocate two buffers, while each request can create an announcement read by many peers. Inbox caps alone do not bound request processing, announcement retention, or fanout. A bounded request budget, admission decision, and observable refusal or timeout are needed. A lagging announcement reader can receive `gap` and miss a call. [revision:49](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:49), [revision:145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:145), [dao.stream.md:674](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:674)\n    14\t\n    15\t## Changed questions\n    16\t\n    17\t- **Q2 — retain the earlier consensus: defer held reads.** Inbox polling adds traffic, but the existing retry interval and attempt budget already prevent a busy loop. Holds trade network traffic for per-session state and repeated polls at M; they are an optional performance choice, not a condition for NAT reachability. If added, bound held demands and work per driver step. [revision:68](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:68), [original design:131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:131), [original design:194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:194)\n    18\t- **Q3 — retain the earlier consensus, clarified:** the peer’s **own composition** mints its opaque random ID; no meeting peer assigns it. A public-key hash without proof of control provides no authenticated peer binding. Discovery’s kickoff-hash rule concerns stream identity and verifies signatures; it is not an existing peer-registration rule. Define duplicate `:meet/here` handling in the meeting convention. [revision:69](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69), [dao.stream.discovery.md:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:70), [dao.stream.md:1087](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:1087)\n    19\t- **Q4 — agree that policy moves to M’s composition; retain the earlier policy’s full scope.** Bound request admission and work, announcements, inbox count and bytes, and idle lifetime. Open admission belongs to explicitly trusted deployments; postage remains a possible public policy. Calling M an ordinary peer does not remove the resources it allocates. [revision:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:70), [dao.stream.discovery.md:159](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:159)\n    20\t\n    21\t**Q1, Q5, and Q7 keep their decisions**, but Q1 must correlate both hops’ append outcomes, Q5 must distinguish an outer inbox gap from a final source gap, and Q7 needs the wider prose correction. **Q6 is operationally affected:** a source’s served-entry lifetime is insufficient if M retires the inbox pair while a migrated task still needs that route. [revision:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55), [revision:158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:158)\n    22\t\n    23\t## Replacement wording for the revision\n    24\t\n    25\t- **§6.2 identity:** “A WebSocket descriptor’s `:dao.stream/identity` names the logical stream exposed at that endpoint. The acceptor’s peer ID is separate reachability data under `:serve/peer`; dialing or accepting confers no stream ownership.” A peer ID cannot silently replace the contract’s logical-stream identity. [revision:127](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:127), [dao.stream.md:263](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:263)\n    26\t- **§7.3 meeting streams:** “M owns the request and announcement media. It serves restricted writer-only and reader-only handles respectively, retaining its own complementary handles. A meeting request has a correlation ID. M admits it under a bounded policy, records its outcome as an announcement, and attributes an observed UDP address only through an explicit association between the request and the UDP channel event; a bare peer-ID claim supplies no address or authority.” [revision:145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:145), [dao.stream.md:389](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:389)\n    27\t- **§6.4 and §7.4:** “An outer inbox gap is channel loss. Advance its outer cursor to the returned recovery cursor and recover outstanding inner requests under their existing IDs; never report that gap as eviction from the final served stream. A punch candidate reuses the UDP socket whose reflexive address M observed. It becomes direct only after a matching nonce-bearing pong confirms the path; otherwise the bounded attempt fails and the inbox path remains available.” [revision:131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:131), [revision:160](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:160)\n    28\t- **§13 criterion 10:** “The same serve and proxy implementation supports every peer; a peer may additionally choose the meeting interpreter and its explicit resource policy. Tests cover two dial-only peers, loss and reordering at both framing layers, bounded meeting work, announcement gaps, and fallback.” This matches the owner’s allowance for a convention without pretending every composition has identical configuration. [revision:184](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:184)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '42,75p;94,162p;175,184p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '42,75p;94,162p;175,184p'\"","aggregated_output":"    42\t## 3. Can relay and rendezvous be ordinary peers? Yes, completely\n    43\t\n    44\tThe mechanism. Any peer M may choose to serve a **meeting stream pair** under identities it announces however it likes (a descriptor in a directory stream, a rendezvous topic in `dao.stream.discovery`, a URL on a wall). The pair is exactly the `dao.stream.ws.md` request-medium pattern, so nothing new is specified:\n    45\t\n    46\t- **`meet-requests`**: writer surface only. Any peer opens a session and appends requests. This is M's request medium; many writers, and the meaning of their order is M's interpreter's.\n    47\t- **`meet-announcements`**: reader surface only, single-writer (M). M's interpreter reads its request medium and appends facts.\n    48\t\n    49\tThree request kinds and three fact kinds, all convention vocabulary under `:meet/…`, none of them serve frames:\n    50\t\n    51\t- `{:meet/here <peer-id>}` → M creates two ring buffers, serves them, and announces `{:meet/inbox <peer-id> :dao.stream/identity <in> …}` with the serve descriptors of the pair. Any peer that reads the announcement stream now knows how to reach that peer through M.\n    52\t- On every request M's interpreter also announces `{:meet/seen <peer-id> :meet/reflexive {:host … :port …}}`, taken from the request's `:udp/attachment` in M's traffic medium. That is the whole of STUN: the reflexive address is a channel fact M already deposits, republished as a value.\n    53\t- `{:meet/call <peer-id> :meet/from <my-peer-id>}` → M announces it. Both parties read the announcement stream anyway, so both learn the other's reflexive address and that a call is in progress. That is the whole of the introduction.\n    54\t\n    55\t**Relayed channel.** A channel is a writer handle plus a reader handle (§6.1 revised). Peer A reaching B through M uses a proxy to B's inbox as its writer and a proxy to its own inbox as its reader. The serve protocol runs unchanged over it: sessions inside, `:serve/open` on B's serve table, cursors verbatim. M runs the ordinary serve-step over ring buffers and never sees a session it did not itself serve. Because the inner protocol already tolerates loss (idempotent reads, deduplicated appends), an inbox that evicts under pressure produces an inner `gap` on the outer proxy, which the inner retry policy absorbs.\n    56\t\n    57\t**Hole punching.** After a call is announced, both peers `attach!` a UDP channel to the other's reflexive address and send `:serve/ping`. The first inbound datagram from that address is deposited by the UDP adapter with that attachment identity; the composition matches it to the pending candidate and the direct channel exists. If no pong arrives in the punch budget, the inbox-through-M channel is the next candidate. No frame beyond `:serve/ping` and `:serve/pong` is involved.\n    58\t\n    59\t**What remains privileged:** nothing at the protocol level. Two facts remain, neither a privilege: M must have a reachable address (a network fact, and exactly why some peer has to be reachable for any two NATed peers to meet), and M must have chosen to run the meeting convention (a composition choice any peer can make). Under the reading in part 1 this satisfies the invariant. It is also stigmergy in the literal sense: peers coordinate through traces left on a shared medium, which is the project's founding model, and the meeting stream is the same object as the discovery document's directory stream.\n    60\t\n    61\tCosts, stated honestly: a relayed message is framed twice (outer session to M, inner session to the peer) and a peer must poll its inbox on M. The second cost is what changes Q2.\n    62\t\n    63\t## 4. Effect of the invariant on the Q1–Q7 consensus\n    64\t\n    65\t| Q | Consensus position | Effect | Revised position |\n    66\t|---|---|---|---|\n    67\t| Q1 | event-medium append; UCF `:put` resumes on observed source outcome; append-unknown leaves the wait undischarged | none; no role is involved | unchanged |\n    68\t| Q2 | defer held reads | **changes** | Include `:serve/hold` in v1, or at the latest in the same milestone as the meeting convention. The consensus deferred it when a relay was a forwarder that pushed frames. With no relay, a peer's reachability through M *is* polling its inbox proxy; held reads are what keep that from being a busy loop across the network. Deferring them now makes the only NAT fallback a chatty one. |\n    69\t| Q3 | opaque composition-assigned ids; duplicate `:serve/register` is relay policy; self-certifying later; public relay needs channel auth | **changes** | There is no assigner. A peer id is **minted by the peer itself**, from the start: a 128-bit random value at minimum, and in the self-certifying form (hash of the peer's public key, `dao.stream.discovery` mechanism 1) as the intended format, with signature verification an additive rule later. \"Composition-assigned\" must not mean assigned by a relay. Duplicate `:meet/here` for one id is an ordinary fact on M's announcement stream; what M's interpreter does with it is M's convention, and which meeting stream a reader trusts is the reader's choice, exactly as with directories. The \"public relay needs channel auth\" tripwire becomes \"a peer serving a public request stream gates it by an interpreter rule\", which the contract already permits. |\n    70\t| Q4 | relay admission is composition policy; lifecycle and caps required; postage future | **moves, substance survives** | There is no relay to admit. Inbox creation, retention and idle unserving are M's serve-table policy over its own request medium, identical to any served writer surface. Caps and lifecycle are required exactly as the consensus said, restated as \"M bounds the number and retention of inboxes it serves\". Postage stays the intended answer for a public meeting stream. |\n    71\t| Q5 | accept OD-2, OD-3, corrected OD-1; amend blocked and anchors | none | unchanged |\n    72\t| Q6 | lease-governed lifetime; retirement as stopgap | none; exporter and resumer are per-migration roles | unchanged |\n    73\t| Q7 | `dao.stream.serve`; rename `dao.stream.serving`; fix ws prose | strengthens | unchanged, plus: retitle §5, delete the relay and rendezvous sections, and rename the copy composition to say \"copy\" or \"broadcast\" so it reads as a convention. |\n    74\t\n    75\tThe consensus is not wrong where it stands; it was reached against a design that had a relay in it. Q2 and Q3 are the two places where removing the relay flips the answer.\n    94\t### §2 Layering (replace the diagram's last two rows and add one rule)\n    95\t\n    96\t```\n    97\tchannel        a writer handle and a reader handle the composition supplies:\n    98\t               a ws or udp attachment with its deposited medium, or two proxied\n    99\t               streams served by a third peer, or two ring buffers in one process\n   100\treachability   how a channel is established: direct, punched, or through a peer\n   101\t               that serves an inbox pair under the meeting convention (§7)\n   102\t```\n   103\t\n   104\tAdd to the rules: **Every peer runs both steps.** `serve-step` answers sessions on the streams in its serve table; `proxy-step` drives the sessions it opened. Every channel, whoever dialed it, is attached to both steps at both ends. There is no server node and no client node; \"serving\" and \"attaching\" are what a peer does in one session.\n   105\t\n   106\t### §3.1 Sessions (replace the identity sentence)\n   107\t\n   108\tA session is identified by `[opener-peer-id n]`, where `n` is unique among the sessions that peer has opened on this channel. Both ends of a channel may open sessions, and a channel may be a shared medium with many opening peers, so the peer id is part of the identity.\n   109\t\n   110\t### §3.2 Frames (delete five, rename none)\n   111\t\n   112\tDelete `:serve/register`, `:serve/dial`, `:serve/introduce`, `:serve/via` and `:serve/punch`. The reachability control block is removed entirely. The frame set is: `:serve/open`, `:serve/opened`, `:serve/disclaim`, `:serve/close`, `:serve/request`, `:serve/response`, `:serve/ping`, `:serve/pong`. Nothing in the protocol names a peer kind.\n   113\t\n   114\t### §5 The serving side: serve-step and the serve table (retitle; body unchanged except wording)\n   115\t\n   116\tReplace \"the server\" with \"the serving side of a session\" throughout, and open the section with: every peer has a serve table and runs `serve-step`; a peer whose table is empty answers every `:serve/open` with `:serve/disclaim` and is otherwise indistinguishable from any other.\n   117\t\n   118\t### §6.1 Channel contract (replace items 1 and 2)\n   119\t\n   120\t1. A **writer handle** whose `append!` places one value on this channel's outbound path.\n   121\t2. A **reader handle** with the contract's reader surface, positioned on this channel's inbound values. A ws or udp attachment supplies it as the medium its adapter deposits into; a proxied stream served by another peer supplies it directly, because a proxy has a reader surface; two ring buffers supply it in process.\n   122\t\n   123\tA channel declares ordering and reliability from its parts: a deposited medium is ordered and best-effort under eviction; a proxied inbox is ordered and best-effort under the inbox's retention; UDP is unordered and best-effort.\n   124\t\n   125\t### §6.2 WebSocket channel (replace the Direction paragraph and host line)\n   126\t\n   127\t**Direction is establishment, not authority.** A WebSocket has a dialer and an acceptor because TCP does; a browser can only dial. The channel descriptor's host, port and path are the acceptor's, and its `:dao.stream/identity` is the acceptor's peer id. Once established, the channel is attached to both steps at both ends and nothing in this layer remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs node and cljd may accept or dial; cljs browser may dial. `dao.stream.serve.ws` wires an attachment, from either side, to `serve-step` and `proxy-step`.\n   128\t\n   129\t### §6.4 A channel over served streams (replaces \"A channel over a channel\")\n   130\t\n   131\tTwo proxied streams are a channel: peer A's writer is a proxy to a stream B reads, and A's reader is a proxy to a stream B writes. When a third peer M serves both streams, A and B have a channel without reaching each other, and M runs nothing but the ordinary serve-step over two ring buffers. Serve frames inside such a channel are values in M's streams and M never interprets them. This is streams as values sent through streams, and it is the whole of relaying.\n   132\t\n   133\t### §7 Reachability (replace §7.1 through §7.4)\n   134\t\n   135\t**7.1 The serve descriptor.** As before, with `:serve/peer` the holding peer's self-minted id, and `:serve/candidates` an ordered vector where each element is one of:\n   136\t\n   137\t- a **channel descriptor** (ws or udp) naming an acceptor address of the holding peer;\n   138\t- a **serve descriptor** for a stream some peer M serves that is the holding peer's inbox, together with the announcement-stream descriptor where M publishes the matching outbox: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/serve :dao.stream/identity <inbox> :serve/peer M :serve/meet <M's announcement-stream descriptor> :serve/candidates [M's channels]}`;\n   139\t- a **meeting resolution** `{:serve/meet <descriptor> :serve/peer <holding-peer-id>}`, meaning: attach to that announcement stream, append `:meet/call` to its request stream, read the holding peer's reflexive address, and attach a UDP channel to it while the holding peer does the same toward mine.\n   140\t\n   141\tThe proxy tries candidates in order; each attempt's resolution is deposited on the proxy's event medium. The descriptor carries no authorization.\n   142\t\n   143\t**7.2 Direct.** A peer with an acceptor channel, a listening ws endpoint or a bound UDP socket, is reached by a channel descriptor. This is establishment: the acceptor holds no authority the dialer lacks.\n   144\t\n   145\t**7.3 The meeting convention.** Any peer M may serve a **request stream** (writer surface; M's request medium, many writers) and an **announcement stream** (reader surface; single writer, M). An interpreter of M's composition reads the request medium and appends to the announcement stream. The vocabulary is a convention, not protocol:\n   146\t\n   147\t```clojure\n   148\t;; requests, appended by any peer through an ordinary session\n   149\t{:meet/here <peer-id>}                                   ; make me reachable through you\n   150\t{:meet/call <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}              ; I want a channel to that peer\n   151\t\n   152\t;; facts, appended by M alone\n   153\t{:meet/inbox <peer-id> :serve/in <serve descriptor> :serve/out <serve descriptor>}\n   154\t{:meet/seen  <peer-id> :meet/reflexive {:host … :port …}}   ; from :udp/attachment on the request\n   155\t{:meet/call  <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}                ; the call, republished\n   156\t```\n   157\t\n   158\tOn `:meet/here`, M creates two ring buffers, adds them to its serve table, and announces their descriptors; the named peer reads `in` and any peer may write it; the reverse for `out`. The pair, proxied, is a channel (§6.4). Retention, the number of pairs M keeps, and when an idle pair is unserved are M's composition policy, the same policy any served writer surface has. M is not a relay; it is a peer serving ring buffers, and a peer that reads M's announcements decides whether to trust them exactly as it decides whose directory to fold in `dao.stream.discovery`.\n   159\t\n   160\t**7.4 Punching.** After a call is announced, each party has the other's reflexive address from `:meet/seen`. Each `attach!`es a UDP channel to it and sends `:serve/ping` on the schedule its driver's `now` allows; the first inbound datagram from that address is deposited with that attachment identity, the composition matches it to the pending candidate, and a direct channel exists. No pong within the punch budget means the next candidate, normally the inbox pair on the same M. A symmetric NAT defeats punching and takes that path; the protocol reports a failed candidate, not a failure.\n   161\t\n   162\t**7.5 P2P symmetry** and **7.6 Liveness** unchanged, except that `:serve/ping` is also the punch probe.\n   175\t### §12 Deferred (replace the authorization item)\n   176\t\n   177\tGating of a public meeting request stream (postage or capability) is an interpreter rule on M's own medium; the contract gates nothing and this layer adds nothing.\n   178\t\n   179\t### §13 Completion criteria (replace items 5 and 6)\n   180\t\n   181\t5. **Reachability without acceptance.** A peer that only dials serves a stream to a peer that only dials, through an inbox pair on a third peer that runs nothing but `serve-step` and the meeting interpreter; the third peer's serve table holds two ring buffers and no other state about the session.\n   182\t6. **Punch and fallback.** Two UDP peers behind a NAT simulator obtain a direct channel through a meeting stream's `:meet/seen` facts and `:serve/ping`; under a symmetric-NAT simulator they fall to the inbox pair, reported as a failed candidate on the proxy's event medium.\n   183\t\n   184\tAdd: 10. **Symmetry proof.** The same peer composition, with no configuration difference beyond which channels it has, plays the accepted-side, dialed-side, inbox-holding and inbox-using positions in items 5 and 6.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790340000000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r1.glm-5.3.findings.md | sed -n '1,110p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790340000000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r1.glm-5.3.findings.md | sed -n '1,110p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:04:09 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:04:09 +07\n     3\t\n     4\t# dao.stream.serve — review of Fable's P2P-invariant revision (GLM-5.3, round 1)\n     5\t\n     6\tVerdict up front: **the dissolution is sound — relay and rendezvous really are derivable as ordinary peers serving ordinary streams, and \"nothing privileged remains\" is true at the protocol level. The mechanism has two real defects (one decisive: the punch socket rule) and one privacy regression.** Of the revised positions: I AGREE on Q2 and Q3 (new evidence, not convergence), AGREE on Q4 with one addition; the earlier consensus should not stand for Q2/Q3 as written. Q1, Q5, Q6, Q7 are genuinely unaffected. I verified every section-5 citation, including `rpc.cljc` `server-state`/`serve-once!` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:100,106` — per-session roles, no change needed, as Fable says), `dao.stream.discovery.md:119-125`, and `dao.jing.dht.md:163-167`.\n     7\t\n     8\t## 1. The mechanism (section 3)\n     9\t\n    10\t**Sound and derivable.** I traced every element to the contract: a writer-only served stream (surface restriction, findings.md:91-99), a reader-only single-writer served stream, appends through ordinary sessions (concurrent writers linearize by the source's sequence, findings.md:278), reads cursor-keyed and idempotent so UDP loss/reorder at the meeting layer is absorbed by the existing design (findings.md:109-117) — Fable's claim that an evicting inbox yields inner `gap`s that the inner retry absorbs checks out, because inner requests/responses are re-derivable by idempotence. The revised frame set (open/opened/disclaim/close/request/response/ping/pong) hides no node kind, and \"every peer runs both steps\" plus the owner's rulings (conventions are not privileges; dialer/acceptor is an establishment fact) close the invariant. Criterion 5's inversion (a dial-only peer serving through an inbox on a dial-only third peer, whose table holds \"two ring buffers and no other state about the session\") is correct. The double framing is stated honestly. **\"Nothing privileged remains\" is true as claimed** — the residual facts (M must be reachable; M chose the convention) are, per the owner's ruling (1), not privileges.\n    11\t\n    12\t**Defect 1 — decisive: the punch must come from the same socket that produced the published reflexive address.** Revised §7.4 says each party \"`attach!`es a UDP channel\" to the other's reflexive address and pings. But `attach!` binds a **fresh local socket** (findings.md:192), and a NAT mapping exists per internal endpoint — a new local port means a new external mapping under **every** NAT type, not just symmetric ones. So the address M published (the mapping of the socket that talked to M) is stale on arrival; the ping that reaches the peer comes from a mapping it was never told, and punching as drafted succeeds only where the *receiving* side's filtering is permissive (full-cone). Classic STUN/ICE works for all cone NATs precisely because checks are sent from the same socket whose mapping was observed. Two required edits: (a) §7.4 — punch from the channel used to reach M, i.e. through the **same socket** whose mapping `:meet/seen` reported; (b) §6.3 — the udp seam must allow sending to an explicit destination on an existing socket, the direct channel being the new attachment that socket acquires when the peer's ping arrives. And criterion 6's NAT simulator must be **restricted-cone**; a full-cone simulator would pass a degraded implementation.\n    13\t\n    14\t**Defect 2 — the reflexive correlation is glossed.** §7.3 says `:meet/seen` is \"from `:udp/attachment` on the request,\" but the attachment identity lives on the **channel's traffic medium**, and the appended request value carries no address; the serve layer \"interprets operations but never values\" (findings.md:273), so nothing stamps it automatically. The meeting interpreter must correlate the appended request with the channel event **by session identity** (both media carry it; sessions are `[opener-peer-id n]`). One sentence fixes it (wording in §4 below).\n    15\t\n    16\t**Regression Fable's table omits — privacy.** The original `:serve/introduce` was addressed to the two peers (findings.md:232); the revision publishes every peer's reflexive address (`:meet/seen`) as facts on a shared, publicly readable announcement stream — a home IP:port exposure to every reader of M's stream. Minimum fix: state it in the honest-costs line; better fix at zero mechanism cost: M delivers the other party's reflexive via each peer's own inbox (`:meet/caller <id> :meet/reflexive {…}` appended to the called peer's `in`), so the shared stream carries only peer ids.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Two smaller requirements.** (i) `:meet/here` is an append — non-idempotent; a lost response (`:serve/append-unknown`, findings.md:115) followed by a re-request on a fresh session can double the inbox. The convention must state the re-read discipline: on unknown, re-read announcements from a cursor minted **before** the append (the contract's mint-before-invoke rule, `dao.stream.md:496-499`) before re-appending. (ii) The announcement stream's retention must exceed intended reader lag (the same sizing relation as `dao.lease.md:259`), or a lagging peer gaps over its own inbox fact and reachability discovery silently degrades.\n    19\t\n    20\t## 2. Q2, Q3, Q4\n    21\t\n    22\t**Q2 — AGREE with the revision; the consensus position should not stand.** This is an evidence-mover, not a concession. My deferral rested on \"network chatter is a policy knob\": in the original design a dialed channel **pushes** inbound frames into a local medium, so a parked reader polls host-locally and only its own demands cost wire. The revision removes that for NAT'd peers: a peer reachable through M has no pushed channel — its inbound path is a proxy over a serve session, so **reachability itself is a standing poll of the inbox proxy**, and inbound-connection latency is bounded by poll cadence. Held reads are the only mechanism in the protocol that lets a server answer later without a new request, turning per-retry traffic into per-budget traffic and restoring push-like delivery over a poll protocol. Two precisions: hold state is per-session and dies with the session (the proxy's demand machinery re-issues — state this, per findings.md:131's session scoping); and the timing should be definite, matching Fable's own second horn — held reads land **with the meeting-convention milestone** (criteria 5-6), not necessarily with serve-core loopback/direct (criteria 1-4, 7, 9 need none of it).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Q3 — AGREE with the revision.** \"Composition-assigned\" (my phrase in the mob, reading findings.md:284) presupposed an assigner; with no relay there is none, and each peer's own composition assigning its id *is* self-minting — the revision disambiguates correctly rather than overturning. Self-minted 128-bit-random minimum makes collisions negligible; hash-of-public-key as the intended format aligns with `dao.stream.discovery.md:70-80` mechanism 1 and with the mob's own r2/r3 trajectory (verification is the authenticated channel's registration handshake, additive). Duplicate `:meet/here` as an ordinary fact handled by M's convention matches the mob's duplicate-registration rule, relocated to where an authority no longer exists. One caveat that must ride along: until the additive verification lands, a self-minted id is still an **unauthenticated name** (`dao.stream.md:1087-1092`) — the intended format must not be read as delivering authentication, and the honest-costs line should say so.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Q4 — AGREE; the consensus substance survives restated.** No relay to admit, but the consensus's content maps exactly: admission is M's interpreter rule over its own request medium (the contract gates nothing, `dao.stream.md:285-292`), caps and lifecycle become \"M bounds the number and retention of inboxes it serves,\" postage remains the intended answer for a public meeting stream (`dao.stream.discovery.md:158-162`, design unspecified at `:231-233`). Add the announcement-retention sizing sentence (item 1(ii) above) to the same policy family.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 3. Q1, Q5, Q6, Q7\n    29\t\n    30\t**Q1 — unaffected, confirmed.** Append semantics, the event-medium outcome, and the UCF `:put` amendment are untouched; a source outcome displaced through a relayed channel is still \"the observed source outcome,\" just observable later.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Q5 — unaffected, confirmed — and reinforced.** The proxy's cached-read semantics are identical, and the revision multiplies the case the amended definitions were written for: an inbox-channel reader is a proxy whose channel legs are themselves proxies, i.e. deferred observation all the way down. The consensus amendment text (orchestrator file, lines 24-36) needs no change.\n    33\t\n    34\t**Q6 — unaffected, with one note.** Exporter and resumer are per-migration roles, covered by the owner's convention clause. The note: the UCF migration acceptance's \"adequate lifetime\" now spans **two** reclamation layers — the exporter's served entry (lease-governed) *and* M's idle-inbox policy; a resumer can lose reachability because M reclaimed, not because a lease lapsed, and it surfaces as a failed candidate / `:serve/disclaim`, not a lease fact. One sentence in §9 or criterion 8, so the acceptance test fixes M's policy for the duration.\n    35\t\n    36\t**Q7 — unaffected, strengthened as Fable says.** The retitle of §5, deletion of the relay/rendezvous sections, and re-describing the copy composition as a broadcast convention are all in the consensus's direction.\n    37\t\n    38\t## 4. Edits to sections 5 and 6 (exact wording)\n    39\t\n    40\t1. **§7.4, replace the first sentence:** \"After a call is announced, each party punches **through the same UDP channel it used to reach M** — the socket whose mapping `:meet/seen` reported — sending `:serve/ping` to the other's reflexive address on the schedule its driver's `now` allows. The udp channel seam therefore permits sending to an explicit destination on an existing socket, and the direct channel is the attachment that socket acquires when the other's ping arrives. **A fresh `attach!` mints a fresh socket and therefore a fresh NAT mapping under every NAT type; punching from a new socket succeeds only against permissive (full-cone) filtering and is not this design's punch.** No pong within the punch budget means the next candidate…\" — and in §13 criterion 6, after \"NAT simulator,\" insert \"(**restricted-cone**, so the same-socket rule is load-bearing)\".\n    41\t2. **§7.3, add after the `:meet/seen` fact:** \"The reflexive address of a request is the channel attachment identity its session arrived on; the meeting interpreter correlates the appended request with that attachment by session identity, which the channel's medium and the request both carry, and publishes the pair. A requester whose `:meet/here` ends `append-unknown` re-reads the announcement stream from a cursor minted before the append before re-appending, so an inbox is not doubled. The announcement stream's retention exceeds the lag of the readers M intends to serve.\"\n    42\t3. **§7.3 honest costs, add:** \"A relayed value is framed twice, a reachable peer holds one demand open against its inbox, and `:meet/seen` facts on a shared announcement stream expose reflexive addresses to every reader — a convention may instead deliver the caller's reflexive through the called peer's own inbox — and until channel authentication exists, a peer id is an unauthenticated name (`dao.stream.md`, *Recorded, not proposed*).\"\n    43\t4. **§7.1, third candidate kind, one-word consistency fix** (the inventory row says punching is \"a resolution rule… not a candidate kind\"): call it \"a candidate whose resolution is the meeting procedure of §7.3,\" so the candidate list and the inventory agree.\n    44\t5. **Section 5 table, add two rows:** (high) original design §7.4 `:serve/introduce` was addressed to the two peers (findings.md:232) while the revision publishes `:meet/seen` to all readers — the privacy regression above; (medium) §7.4 punch socket — the same-socket rule, without which criterion 6 is satisfiable by a degraded implementation.\n    45\t\n    46\tI hold these on evidence, not to converge: the mechanism is the right shape, the invariant reading is faithful to the owner's rulings, and the defects above are fixable by the wording given without reopening any settled question.\n    47\t\n    48\tCONSENSUS (my positions this round): mechanism sound with the five edits; Q2 revised-include (meeting-convention milestone), Q3 self-minted, Q4 restated — consensus Q2/Q3 superseded, Q4 substance survives; Q1/Q5/Q6/Q7 unaffected.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '35,185p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '35,185p'\"","aggregated_output":"    35\t| low | 267-274, §10 \"Relay and rendezvous interpret nothing\" | Follows. | Line removed. |\n    36\t| low | 304-312, Q3, Q4 as written | Both presume a relay authority. | Revised in part 4. |\n    37\t| none | 55-78, `:serve/open`, `:serve/opened`, `:serve/disclaim` | Per-session asymmetry: one side holds the stream. Permitted. | Unchanged; note it as a session fact. |\n    38\t| none | 236-238, §7.5 | Already symmetric. | Unchanged. |\n    39\t\n    40\tWhat stays as a per-channel establishment fact and is never an authority: which end listened and which dialed (WebSocket); whose socket address is written in a channel descriptor; that a browser can only dial. After establishment, every channel is attached to both `serve-step` and `proxy-step` on both ends.\n    41\t\n    42\t## 3. Can relay and rendezvous be ordinary peers? Yes, completely\n    43\t\n    44\tThe mechanism. Any peer M may choose to serve a **meeting stream pair** under identities it announces however it likes (a descriptor in a directory stream, a rendezvous topic in `dao.stream.discovery`, a URL on a wall). The pair is exactly the `dao.stream.ws.md` request-medium pattern, so nothing new is specified:\n    45\t\n    46\t- **`meet-requests`**: writer surface only. Any peer opens a session and appends requests. This is M's request medium; many writers, and the meaning of their order is M's interpreter's.\n    47\t- **`meet-announcements`**: reader surface only, single-writer (M). M's interpreter reads its request medium and appends facts.\n    48\t\n    49\tThree request kinds and three fact kinds, all convention vocabulary under `:meet/…`, none of them serve frames:\n    50\t\n    51\t- `{:meet/here <peer-id>}` → M creates two ring buffers, serves them, and announces `{:meet/inbox <peer-id> :dao.stream/identity <in> …}` with the serve descriptors of the pair. Any peer that reads the announcement stream now knows how to reach that peer through M.\n    52\t- On every request M's interpreter also announces `{:meet/seen <peer-id> :meet/reflexive {:host … :port …}}`, taken from the request's `:udp/attachment` in M's traffic medium. That is the whole of STUN: the reflexive address is a channel fact M already deposits, republished as a value.\n    53\t- `{:meet/call <peer-id> :meet/from <my-peer-id>}` → M announces it. Both parties read the announcement stream anyway, so both learn the other's reflexive address and that a call is in progress. That is the whole of the introduction.\n    54\t\n    55\t**Relayed channel.** A channel is a writer handle plus a reader handle (§6.1 revised). Peer A reaching B through M uses a proxy to B's inbox as its writer and a proxy to its own inbox as its reader. The serve protocol runs unchanged over it: sessions inside, `:serve/open` on B's serve table, cursors verbatim. M runs the ordinary serve-step over ring buffers and never sees a session it did not itself serve. Because the inner protocol already tolerates loss (idempotent reads, deduplicated appends), an inbox that evicts under pressure produces an inner `gap` on the outer proxy, which the inner retry policy absorbs.\n    56\t\n    57\t**Hole punching.** After a call is announced, both peers `attach!` a UDP channel to the other's reflexive address and send `:serve/ping`. The first inbound datagram from that address is deposited by the UDP adapter with that attachment identity; the composition matches it to the pending candidate and the direct channel exists. If no pong arrives in the punch budget, the inbox-through-M channel is the next candidate. No frame beyond `:serve/ping` and `:serve/pong` is involved.\n    58\t\n    59\t**What remains privileged:** nothing at the protocol level. Two facts remain, neither a privilege: M must have a reachable address (a network fact, and exactly why some peer has to be reachable for any two NATed peers to meet), and M must have chosen to run the meeting convention (a composition choice any peer can make). Under the reading in part 1 this satisfies the invariant. It is also stigmergy in the literal sense: peers coordinate through traces left on a shared medium, which is the project's founding model, and the meeting stream is the same object as the discovery document's directory stream.\n    60\t\n    61\tCosts, stated honestly: a relayed message is framed twice (outer session to M, inner session to the peer) and a peer must poll its inbox on M. The second cost is what changes Q2.\n    62\t\n    63\t## 4. Effect of the invariant on the Q1–Q7 consensus\n    64\t\n    65\t| Q | Consensus position | Effect | Revised position |\n    66\t|---|---|---|---|\n    67\t| Q1 | event-medium append; UCF `:put` resumes on observed source outcome; append-unknown leaves the wait undischarged | none; no role is involved | unchanged |\n    68\t| Q2 | defer held reads | **changes** | Include `:serve/hold` in v1, or at the latest in the same milestone as the meeting convention. The consensus deferred it when a relay was a forwarder that pushed frames. With no relay, a peer's reachability through M *is* polling its inbox proxy; held reads are what keep that from being a busy loop across the network. Deferring them now makes the only NAT fallback a chatty one. |\n    69\t| Q3 | opaque composition-assigned ids; duplicate `:serve/register` is relay policy; self-certifying later; public relay needs channel auth | **changes** | There is no assigner. A peer id is **minted by the peer itself**, from the start: a 128-bit random value at minimum, and in the self-certifying form (hash of the peer's public key, `dao.stream.discovery` mechanism 1) as the intended format, with signature verification an additive rule later. \"Composition-assigned\" must not mean assigned by a relay. Duplicate `:meet/here` for one id is an ordinary fact on M's announcement stream; what M's interpreter does with it is M's convention, and which meeting stream a reader trusts is the reader's choice, exactly as with directories. The \"public relay needs channel auth\" tripwire becomes \"a peer serving a public request stream gates it by an interpreter rule\", which the contract already permits. |\n    70\t| Q4 | relay admission is composition policy; lifecycle and caps required; postage future | **moves, substance survives** | There is no relay to admit. Inbox creation, retention and idle unserving are M's serve-table policy over its own request medium, identical to any served writer surface. Caps and lifecycle are required exactly as the consensus said, restated as \"M bounds the number and retention of inboxes it serves\". Postage stays the intended answer for a public meeting stream. |\n    71\t| Q5 | accept OD-2, OD-3, corrected OD-1; amend blocked and anchors | none | unchanged |\n    72\t| Q6 | lease-governed lifetime; retirement as stopgap | none; exporter and resumer are per-migration roles | unchanged |\n    73\t| Q7 | `dao.stream.serve`; rename `dao.stream.serving`; fix ws prose | strengthens | unchanged, plus: retitle §5, delete the relay and rendezvous sections, and rename the copy composition to say \"copy\" or \"broadcast\" so it reads as a convention. |\n    74\t\n    75\tThe consensus is not wrong where it stands; it was reached against a design that had a relay in it. Q2 and Q3 are the two places where removing the relay flips the answer.\n    76\t\n    77\t## 5. Contradictions with existing docs under the invariant\n    78\t\n    79\t| Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\n    80\t|---|---|---|---|\n    81\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:9-16` | \"the stream's identity and lifecycle belong to the server, not to the wire\"; \"server-hosted stream\" | Identity and lifecycle belong to the peer that holds the stream. Acceptor versus dialer is a channel establishment fact. Reword; the state machine already permits either end to hold the served stream. |\n    82\t| high | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:18-26` | \"serving is what makes any stream remotely attachable\" | Still the copy-model claim flagged before; under the invariant add that the copy composition is one convention, not the transport's meaning of serving. |\n    83\t| medium | `src/cljc/dao/stream/serving.cljc:1-11, 234-237` | \"Host-owned WebSocket serving composition\"; forwards the source from `:forward-anchor` into every accepted socket | Rename to a copy or broadcast name; its docstring should say it implements a client/server broadcast convention over the symmetric transport. |\n    84\t| medium | `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:32-45, 336-371` | \"client handle\", \"server-side accepted-connection handle\", \"the serving host closes… the client\" | Vocabulary: dialer-side and acceptor-side handles; the holder of the stream closes it. |\n    85\t| low | `docs/design/dao.stream.md:594-600` | \"a server-side accepted-connection handle is an attachment and does not gain ownership merely by being on the serving host\" | Already the right principle. Reword \"server-side\" and \"serving host\" to \"acceptor-side\" and \"the host holding the stream\" so the contract does not lend the words authority. |\n    86\t| low | `docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:120-125` | rendezvous topics: \"the neighborhood stores but never authors\" | Not a contradiction; a dependency. The meeting convention is a single-writer announcement stream plus a request medium, and a rendezvous topic is where its descriptor is found. Cite the serve spec from here and adopt mechanism 1 for peer ids (Q3). |\n    87\t| low | `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:165-167` | \"hole-punching, relay/TURN, and bootstrap discovery\" | \"relay/TURN\" becomes \"an inbox pair served by any reachable peer\"; a DHT node is a natural peer to run the meeting convention. |\n    88\t| low | `src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:100-110`, `docs/design/dao.stream.apply.md` | `server-state`, `serve-once!` | Per-session RPC roles, not node kinds. No change needed; note it. |\n    89\t\n    90\t## 6. Revised draft spec sections (ready text)\n    91\t\n    92\tOnly the sections that change. Section numbers match the design.\n    93\t\n    94\t### §2 Layering (replace the diagram's last two rows and add one rule)\n    95\t\n    96\t```\n    97\tchannel        a writer handle and a reader handle the composition supplies:\n    98\t               a ws or udp attachment with its deposited medium, or two proxied\n    99\t               streams served by a third peer, or two ring buffers in one process\n   100\treachability   how a channel is established: direct, punched, or through a peer\n   101\t               that serves an inbox pair under the meeting convention (§7)\n   102\t```\n   103\t\n   104\tAdd to the rules: **Every peer runs both steps.** `serve-step` answers sessions on the streams in its serve table; `proxy-step` drives the sessions it opened. Every channel, whoever dialed it, is attached to both steps at both ends. There is no server node and no client node; \"serving\" and \"attaching\" are what a peer does in one session.\n   105\t\n   106\t### §3.1 Sessions (replace the identity sentence)\n   107\t\n   108\tA session is identified by `[opener-peer-id n]`, where `n` is unique among the sessions that peer has opened on this channel. Both ends of a channel may open sessions, and a channel may be a shared medium with many opening peers, so the peer id is part of the identity.\n   109\t\n   110\t### §3.2 Frames (delete five, rename none)\n   111\t\n   112\tDelete `:serve/register`, `:serve/dial`, `:serve/introduce`, `:serve/via` and `:serve/punch`. The reachability control block is removed entirely. The frame set is: `:serve/open`, `:serve/opened`, `:serve/disclaim`, `:serve/close`, `:serve/request`, `:serve/response`, `:serve/ping`, `:serve/pong`. Nothing in the protocol names a peer kind.\n   113\t\n   114\t### §5 The serving side: serve-step and the serve table (retitle; body unchanged except wording)\n   115\t\n   116\tReplace \"the server\" with \"the serving side of a session\" throughout, and open the section with: every peer has a serve table and runs `serve-step`; a peer whose table is empty answers every `:serve/open` with `:serve/disclaim` and is otherwise indistinguishable from any other.\n   117\t\n   118\t### §6.1 Channel contract (replace items 1 and 2)\n   119\t\n   120\t1. A **writer handle** whose `append!` places one value on this channel's outbound path.\n   121\t2. A **reader handle** with the contract's reader surface, positioned on this channel's inbound values. A ws or udp attachment supplies it as the medium its adapter deposits into; a proxied stream served by another peer supplies it directly, because a proxy has a reader surface; two ring buffers supply it in process.\n   122\t\n   123\tA channel declares ordering and reliability from its parts: a deposited medium is ordered and best-effort under eviction; a proxied inbox is ordered and best-effort under the inbox's retention; UDP is unordered and best-effort.\n   124\t\n   125\t### §6.2 WebSocket channel (replace the Direction paragraph and host line)\n   126\t\n   127\t**Direction is establishment, not authority.** A WebSocket has a dialer and an acceptor because TCP does; a browser can only dial. The channel descriptor's host, port and path are the acceptor's, and its `:dao.stream/identity` is the acceptor's peer id. Once established, the channel is attached to both steps at both ends and nothing in this layer remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs node and cljd may accept or dial; cljs browser may dial. `dao.stream.serve.ws` wires an attachment, from either side, to `serve-step` and `proxy-step`.\n   128\t\n   129\t### §6.4 A channel over served streams (replaces \"A channel over a channel\")\n   130\t\n   131\tTwo proxied streams are a channel: peer A's writer is a proxy to a stream B reads, and A's reader is a proxy to a stream B writes. When a third peer M serves both streams, A and B have a channel without reaching each other, and M runs nothing but the ordinary serve-step over two ring buffers. Serve frames inside such a channel are values in M's streams and M never interprets them. This is streams as values sent through streams, and it is the whole of relaying.\n   132\t\n   133\t### §7 Reachability (replace §7.1 through §7.4)\n   134\t\n   135\t**7.1 The serve descriptor.** As before, with `:serve/peer` the holding peer's self-minted id, and `:serve/candidates` an ordered vector where each element is one of:\n   136\t\n   137\t- a **channel descriptor** (ws or udp) naming an acceptor address of the holding peer;\n   138\t- a **serve descriptor** for a stream some peer M serves that is the holding peer's inbox, together with the announcement-stream descriptor where M publishes the matching outbox: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/serve :dao.stream/identity <inbox> :serve/peer M :serve/meet <M's announcement-stream descriptor> :serve/candidates [M's channels]}`;\n   139\t- a **meeting resolution** `{:serve/meet <descriptor> :serve/peer <holding-peer-id>}`, meaning: attach to that announcement stream, append `:meet/call` to its request stream, read the holding peer's reflexive address, and attach a UDP channel to it while the holding peer does the same toward mine.\n   140\t\n   141\tThe proxy tries candidates in order; each attempt's resolution is deposited on the proxy's event medium. The descriptor carries no authorization.\n   142\t\n   143\t**7.2 Direct.** A peer with an acceptor channel, a listening ws endpoint or a bound UDP socket, is reached by a channel descriptor. This is establishment: the acceptor holds no authority the dialer lacks.\n   144\t\n   145\t**7.3 The meeting convention.** Any peer M may serve a **request stream** (writer surface; M's request medium, many writers) and an **announcement stream** (reader surface; single writer, M). An interpreter of M's composition reads the request medium and appends to the announcement stream. The vocabulary is a convention, not protocol:\n   146\t\n   147\t```clojure\n   148\t;; requests, appended by any peer through an ordinary session\n   149\t{:meet/here <peer-id>}                                   ; make me reachable through you\n   150\t{:meet/call <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}              ; I want a channel to that peer\n   151\t\n   152\t;; facts, appended by M alone\n   153\t{:meet/inbox <peer-id> :serve/in <serve descriptor> :serve/out <serve descriptor>}\n   154\t{:meet/seen  <peer-id> :meet/reflexive {:host … :port …}}   ; from :udp/attachment on the request\n   155\t{:meet/call  <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}                ; the call, republished\n   156\t```\n   157\t\n   158\tOn `:meet/here`, M creates two ring buffers, adds them to its serve table, and announces their descriptors; the named peer reads `in` and any peer may write it; the reverse for `out`. The pair, proxied, is a channel (§6.4). Retention, the number of pairs M keeps, and when an idle pair is unserved are M's composition policy, the same policy any served writer surface has. M is not a relay; it is a peer serving ring buffers, and a peer that reads M's announcements decides whether to trust them exactly as it decides whose directory to fold in `dao.stream.discovery`.\n   159\t\n   160\t**7.4 Punching.** After a call is announced, each party has the other's reflexive address from `:meet/seen`. Each `attach!`es a UDP channel to it and sends `:serve/ping` on the schedule its driver's `now` allows; the first inbound datagram from that address is deposited with that attachment identity, the composition matches it to the pending candidate, and a direct channel exists. No pong within the punch budget means the next candidate, normally the inbox pair on the same M. A symmetric NAT defeats punching and takes that path; the protocol reports a failed candidate, not a failure.\n   161\t\n   162\t**7.5 P2P symmetry** and **7.6 Liveness** unchanged, except that `:serve/ping` is also the punch probe.\n   163\t\n   164\t### §8 Host isolation (replace three rows)\n   165\t\n   166\t| Piece | clj | cljs browser | cljs node | cljd |\n   167\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   168\t| serve frames, proxy-step, serve-step, meeting interpreter | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n   169\t| ws channel, dial | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n   170\t| ws channel, accept | ✓ | — | ✓ | ✓ |\n   171\t| channel over served streams (§6.4) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n   172\t\n   173\tA browser peer is a full peer: it serves and attaches over any channel it can dial, and is reachable by any peer through an inbox pair on a peer it has dialed.\n   174\t\n   175\t### §12 Deferred (replace the authorization item)\n   176\t\n   177\tGating of a public meeting request stream (postage or capability) is an interpreter rule on M's own medium; the contract gates nothing and this layer adds nothing.\n   178\t\n   179\t### §13 Completion criteria (replace items 5 and 6)\n   180\t\n   181\t5. **Reachability without acceptance.** A peer that only dials serves a stream to a peer that only dials, through an inbox pair on a third peer that runs nothing but `serve-step` and the meeting interpreter; the third peer's serve table holds two ring buffers and no other state about the session.\n   182\t6. **Punch and fallback.** Two UDP peers behind a NAT simulator obtain a direct channel through a meeting stream's `:meet/seen` facts and `:serve/ping`; under a symmetric-NAT simulator they fall to the inbox pair, reported as a failed candidate on the proxy's event medium.\n   183\t\n   184\tAdd: 10. **Symmetry proof.** The same peer composition, with no configuration difference beyond which channels it has, plays the accepted-side, dialed-side, inbox-holding and inbox-using positions in items 5 and 6.\n   185\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '260,295p;385,435p;490,505p;667,683p;1082,1095p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '260,295p;385,435p;490,505p;667,683p;1082,1095p'\"","aggregated_output":"   260\t\n   261\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   262\t  key.\n   263\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   264\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   265\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   266\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   267\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   268\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   269\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   270\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   271\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   272\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   273\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   274\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   275\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   276\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   277\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   278\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   279\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   280\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   281\t  attachment is displaced onto another channel, the same value correlates it\n   282\t  there. The key and correlation rule are the contract's; minting,\n   283\t  representation, and the server-side delivery mechanism are the\n   284\t  transport's. Transports that cannot distinguish attachments omit it.\n   285\t- It carries no authorization. Nothing in this contract gates who may attach:\n   286\t  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n   287\t  Whether attachment should be gated at all, and by what, is a question this\n   288\t  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n   289\t  held open for an undesigned mechanism constrains that mechanism's design\n   290\t  from a document with no business specifying it. Should gating arrive, it\n   291\t  arrives as an addition, which the open-map rule and this section's own\n   292\t  transport-owned keys already permit.\n   293\t\n   294\tFor the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`\n   295\ton a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,\n   385\tregistry and no namespace-load registration side effect.\n   386\t\n   387\t## Surfaces\n   388\t\n   389\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   390\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   391\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   392\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   393\t\n   394\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   395\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   396\tAn operation a handle does not\n   397\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   398\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   399\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   400\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   401\toperation exists.\n   402\t\n   403\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   404\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   405\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   406\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   407\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   408\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   409\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   410\t\n   411\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   412\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   413\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   414\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   415\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   416\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   417\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   418\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   419\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   420\t\n   421\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   422\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   423\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   424\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   425\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   426\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   427\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   428\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   429\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   430\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   431\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   432\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n   433\tconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\n   434\tspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n   435\t\n   490\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   491\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   492\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   493\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   494\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   495\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   496\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   497\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   498\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   499\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   500\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   501\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   502\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   503\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   504\t  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n   505\t  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n   667\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   668\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   669\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   670\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   671\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n   672\treceives a `gap`.\n   673\t\n   674\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   675\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   676\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   677\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   678\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   679\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   680\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   681\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   682\t\n   683\t### Complete history\n  1082\t  no cursor. Read-your-writes and causal references between streams arrive as\n  1083\t  an optional `:dao.stream/cursor` key on `ok`. Conditional append (\"only if\n  1084\t  the tail is still here\") would be an eighth operation; the design does not\n  1085\t  need it because it assumes **one writer per logical stream**, which\n  1086\t  `dao.space` practices and this document should say once in *Concurrency*.\n  1087\t- **History is not verifiable.** Identity is an opaque value nothing\n  1088\t  authenticates, and append-only is the transport's promise, not something a\n  1089\t  reader can check. Self-certifying identities and signed or hash-chained\n  1090\t  elements live in the payload, above the stream. The obligation on this layer\n  1091\t  is only the one it already meets: identity is compared structurally and never\n  1092\t  parsed.\n  1093\t- **Readiness.** Polling is O(n) in streams observed (*What it costs*). The\n  1094\t  extension is reserved, and `dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md` pays\n  1095\t  the cost in the runtime without it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '90,137p;188,198p;225,237p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '90,137p;188,198p;225,237p'\"","aggregated_output":"    90\t\n    91\tThe server for a handle `h` with declared surface `S` is the apply handler table below, restricted to `S`. Nothing about `h`'s transport appears anywhere; a string-backed stream, a ring buffer and a durable log are served by the same six lines.\n    92\t\n    93\t| Wire op                | Args                     | Server computes                          | `:dao.stream.apply/ok` value                              |\n    94\t|------------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|\n    95\t| `:dao.stream/descriptor` | `[]`                   | `(descriptor h)`                         | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    96\t| `:dao.stream/cursor`   | `[anchor]`               | `(cursor h anchor)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    97\t| `:dao.stream/next`     | `[cursor budget]`        | `next` repeated from `cursor`, following each `ok`'s successor, at most `budget` times, stopping at the first non-`ok` | a vector of outcome maps, in order, verbatim |\n    98\t| `:dao.stream/append!`  | `[value]`                | `(append! h value)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    99\t| `:dao.stream/close!`   | `[]`                     | `(close! h)` only if `h` is an attachment the server minted for this session; never the owner's close | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}` |\n   100\t\n   101\tAn op absent from `S` is answered with an apply **error** response, code `:dao.stream.serve/no-surface`: the contract forbids answering a nonexistent operation with an outcome map (Surfaces), and the apply envelope has an error shape for exactly this. The proxy never exposes such an op, so the error only ever reaches a misbehaving client.\n   102\t\n   103\tOutcome maps cross **verbatim**, including the cursors inside them. The serve layer never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor. This is the whole basis of cursor-namespace preservation: the proxy hands the reader exactly the cursor the source minted, and hands the source exactly the cursor the reader held.\n   104\t\n   105\t**Precondition on the source.** Its cursors and outcome maps must lie in the channel's portable value domain. Both existing transports qualify (a string identity and an integer position). A transport whose cursor holds a host object cannot be served; the exporter refuses it before publishing (§9). This is the whole content of the UCF's \"portable cursor profile\": one profile, `:dao.stream.serve/v1`, meaning *cursor is plain data in the portable domain and the source honors it after serialization*.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 3.4 Idempotence, loss and duplication\n   108\t\n   109\tFour of the five operations are idempotent as the contract defines them: `descriptor` and `cursor` are projections; `next` is non-destructive and positioned by its argument; `close!` is idempotent by definition. A lost request is re-sent; a lost response is re-requested; a duplicated request re-computes the same answer or a later, equally true one. **No sequence numbers, acknowledgement vectors or sliding windows are needed for reads: the cursor is the sequence number, and `gap` remains the source's declaration rather than a transport artifact.** This is what reconciles UDP with kept cursors and gap, and why v1 DRDS (`daostream-udp-design.md`) is not revived.\n   110\t\n   111\t`append!` is the one non-idempotent operation. The protocol makes it safe by the rule OD-2 already states — deduplication and correlation are the payload's — applied at the serve layer so that no application has to:\n   112\t\n   113\t- A session has **at most one outstanding `append!`**. The proxy does not issue the next until the previous is answered or the session is lost. This is `dao.stream.rpc`'s `unsent` discipline, and it also makes UDP reordering harmless for writes.\n   114\t- The server keeps, per session, the id and outcome of the **last** `append!` it performed. A request with that id is answered from the record without re-appending. Window size one follows from the one-outstanding rule.\n   115\t- If the session is lost with an append unanswered, the proxy reports it as `:dao.stream.serve/append-unknown` on its event medium with the retained value. The proxy does not silently retry across sessions: the far end may have appended. This is OD-2's third state named honestly.\n   116\t\n   117\tA stale response (an id no longer outstanding) is a diagnostic, not an error, exactly as in `dao.stream.rpc`.\n   118\t\n   119\t### 3.5 Batching\n   120\t\n   121\t`next`'s `budget` bounds one response. The server answers with consecutive outcomes; the proxy installs each `(cursor_i → outcome_i)` with `cursor_0` the requested cursor and `cursor_{i+1}` the successor in `outcome_i`. A server also bounds the batch by the channel's frame budget (§6.3): it stops early when the next value would not fit and returns what it has. A single value that cannot fit in one frame on this channel is answered as `transport-error` with `:dao.stream.serve/reason :oversize` in place of that element; the read is not silently skipped and the cursor does not advance past it. Large media belong in the payload as `dao.jing` addresses (contract, Granularity).\n   122\t\n   123\t### 3.6 Anchors across a hop\n   124\t\n   125\t`cursor` has no `blocked` outcome, yet a proxy cannot consult the source synchronously. The design does not invent a proxy-namespaced cursor (that would put a cursor in circulation that the source's own handle rejects, violating \"any handle on the same logical stream accepts it\"). Instead the server **piggybacks fresh `:oldest` and `:newest` cursors on `:serve/opened` and on every response**, and the proxy's `cursor` answers with the most recently observed one.\n   126\t\n   127\tThis is honest under the contract's own definitions. A stale `:newest` is *earlier* than a fresh one, so a composition that mints `:newest` before invoking an operation still observes everything the operation causes; it may additionally observe a few values appended between the last exchange and the mint, which is re-seeing, never skipping. A stale `:oldest` may point at a position since evicted, and the contract already says a fresh `:oldest` is a position rather than a completeness claim: the reader receives `gap` with the recovery cursor. Before the first `:serve/opened` the proxy holds no anchors and `cursor` answers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true` (OD-1's proposed key); the composition observes `:serve/opened` on the proxy's event medium and mints then.\n   128\t\n   129\t### 3.7 Held reads (optional, v1 decision needed)\n   130\t\n   131\tAcross a network a `blocked` answer costs a round trip. A `next` request may carry `:serve/hold true`; a server that honors it records the demand in session state and, on each of its own driver steps, re-polls the source, answering when the outcome is no longer `blocked` or when its hold budget (a constructor policy in the driver's clock domain) elapses, in which case it answers the `blocked` it has. No operation waits: the session holds state, the step returns. A proxy must tolerate a server that ignores the flag. Whether v1 includes this is an owner decision (Q2).\n   132\t\n   133\t## 4. The proxy handle: declared nature\n   134\t\n   135\tA proxy is a handle minted by `attach!` on a serve descriptor. It declares exactly the surface the `:serve/opened` frame reports, which is the source's declared surface; until then it declares the surface the descriptor's `:serve/surface` hint names, and a mismatch at open is a session failure deposited as data. `descriptor` returns the serve descriptor and the identity. The proxy is composed as three explicit pieces: the handle (an object over an explicit state atom, as `WsHandle` is), an **event medium** the composition wires and the channel adapter deposits into, and `proxy-step`, which the composition's driver calls with `now`.\n   136\t\n   137\tThe handle's `next` is a read of the proxy's **window**, a bounded host-local cache keyed by cursor value (structural equality; every cursor a reader holds was minted by the source, through this proxy or another). A miss records a **demand** in proxy state and answers `blocked`. `proxy-step` issues demands as requests, installs responses into the window, opens sessions, retries by the composition's policy, and evicts the window oldest-first. The window is a cache, not retention: an evicted window entry is re-fetched, and only the source ever answers `gap`.\n   188\t### 6.3 UDP channel\n   189\t\n   190\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the ws deposit model and no reader surface: a socket retains nothing. It is the datagram boundary the DHT node already uses, reshaped into the adapter form.\n   191\t\n   192\t- **Descriptor.** `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity <peer-id> :udp/host … :udp/port …}`. `attach!` binds a local socket (ephemeral port) and returns a handle whose writer path is \"datagrams to that address\". No handshake is needed at this layer; the serve layer's `:serve/open` is the handshake.\n   193\t- **One value per datagram**, encoded with the CBOR codec profile (binary, canonical, already specified in ws). Frame budget 1200 bytes, the DHT's figure, chosen to clear common path MTUs without fragmentation. A value over budget is `invalid-value` on `append!` and, from the server's side, `:oversize` (§3.5). IP fragmentation is not relied on; application fragmentation is deferred and, if added, lives in this channel, invisible to the serve layer.\n   194\t- **Declared nature:** `:unordered`, `:best-effort`. The proxy's retry policy therefore has real work: each outstanding request records `:sent-at`; `proxy-step` re-sends when `now - sent-at` exceeds `:retry-after-ms`, with `:max-attempts`, both composition data. Exhaustion is session loss, deposited as data.\n   195\t- **Lifecycle events** are thinner than ws: there is no peer close, so `:udp/closed` is deposited only on local close or deposit failure; peer silence is the serve layer's business (`:serve/ping` and the retry policy), not the channel's.\n   196\t- **Attachment identity** is the remote `(host, port)` pair as seen by the socket; replies go to the datagram's source address, never to a claimed address in the payload, as the DHT already rules.\n   197\t- Hosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs node (`dgram`), cljd (`RawDatagramSocket`). **Browsers have no UDP.** The browser's P2P path is a WebSocket to a relay (§7.3) today and a WebRTC DataChannel channel later (§14); both slot into §6.1 without touching the serve layer.\n   198\t\n   225\t\n   226\tA host behind NAT dials a **relay** R (any host with a reachable address running `relay-step`) and sends `:serve/register` with its peer id, then keeps the channel alive with `:serve/ping`. A client dials R and sends `:serve/dial` naming the peer and a link id it minted. R's state is `{:peers {peer-id → channel-writer} :links {link → [writer-a writer-b]}}`; on `:serve/dial` it pairs the link with the registered peer's channel and thereafter forwards `:serve/via` frames between the two writers by link, copying and judging nothing. Both sides then run the serve protocol over the §6.4 channel. R never sees a session or an operation. A relay is a forwarder: its step is `forward-step` with a link-keyed destination.\n   227\t\n   228\tThis is the browser's whole NAT story and every host's fallback. Its costs are honest: the relay carries every byte, and a registered peer is reachable only while its outbound channel lives, so `:serve/ping` cadence is bounded by NAT binding lifetime (tens of seconds for UDP, longer for TCP). A relay is composition, not infrastructure: anyone runs one, and `dao.stream.discovery`'s rendezvous topics are how a peer finds one.\n   229\t\n   230\t### 7.4 Rendezvous and UDP hole punching\n   231\t\n   232\tBoth peers behind NAT, both with UDP. Each registers with a rendezvous S over UDP; S records the **reflexive address** (source host and port as S observed them) per peer. On `:serve/dial`, S sends `:serve/introduce` to both peers carrying the other's reflexive address and the link. Each peer then sends `:serve/punch` probes with the link's nonce to the other's reflexive address on a schedule `proxy-step` reads from `now`; the first probe to arrive opens the NAT binding in the other direction, and the first `:serve/punch` received is answered with a `:serve/pong`. When both sides have a pong the direct udp channel exists and the serve session opens over it. If no pong arrives within the punch budget, the proxy falls to the next candidate, normally the relay through the same S (a rendezvous is also a relay, and one channel to it serves both roles).\n   233\t\n   234\tThis is STUN and ICE reduced to what the datom.world model needs: reflexive addresses are facts deposited by S, probes are values, and the state machine is a step. Symmetric NATs that assign a fresh port per destination defeat punching and take the relay path; that is a property of the network, reported as a failed candidate, not a failure of the protocol.\n   235\t\n   236\t### 7.5 P2P symmetry\n   237\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790340000000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r1.gpt-6-sol.findings.md | sed -n '1,125p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790340000000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r1.gpt-6-sol.findings.md | sed -n '1,125p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:05:29 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:05:29 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\t## Mechanism\n     5\t\n     6\t**The meeting convention is permitted by the owner’s rulings, but the revised NAT path is not yet mechanically derived from DaoStream alone.** Every peer can run the same serve protocol, and a reachable peer may choose to host meeting streams. That removes a privileged *protocol node kind*. The meeting peer still performs routing, address observation, allocation, and fanout as a composition with costs and policy that must be specified. [revision:42](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:42), [revision:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55)\n     7\t\n     8\tFour gaps prevent the claimed mechanical result:\n     9\t\n    10\t- **Surfaces and provenance.** The serving rule exposes the supplied handle’s declared surface. The meeting interpreter needs private read access to requests and private write access to announcements, while visitors get the opposite surfaces; that requires explicit restricted handles. More seriously, a `:meet/here` value in the request medium does not contain the UDP attachment identity that §3 says M republishes as `:meet/seen`. DaoStream’s attachment correlation is delivered to the composition, not automatically inserted into appended values. [original design:91](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:91), [revision:46](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:46), [revision:52](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:52), [dao.stream.md:272](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:272)\n    11\t- **UDP punching.** The observed reflexive address must belong to the *same bound UDP socket* used for probes. The original UDP `attach!` binds an ephemeral socket; a fresh attachment to the other peer can use another port. A matching nonce-bearing pong, rather than the first inbound datagram, must establish bidirectional reachability. [original design:192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:192), [revision:160](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:160)\n    12\t- **Nested loss and size.** Two proxied streams can carry inner serve frames, but an outer inbox `gap` is loss of *channel frames*, not a `gap` in the final served stream. The inner driver needs a bounded recovery rule for lost or reordered control frames and append-unknown across sessions. Double framing also reduces the UDP payload budget; the existing design refuses an oversized single value rather than making every stream value transferable. [revision:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55), [original design:113](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113), [original design:121](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:121), [original design:193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:193)\n    13\t- **Fairness.** One accepted `:meet/here` can allocate two buffers, while each request can create an announcement read by many peers. Inbox caps alone do not bound request processing, announcement retention, or fanout. A bounded request budget, admission decision, and observable refusal or timeout are needed. A lagging announcement reader can receive `gap` and miss a call. [revision:49](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:49), [revision:145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:145), [dao.stream.md:674](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:674)\n    14\t\n    15\t## Changed questions\n    16\t\n    17\t- **Q2 — retain the earlier consensus: defer held reads.** Inbox polling adds traffic, but the existing retry interval and attempt budget already prevent a busy loop. Holds trade network traffic for per-session state and repeated polls at M; they are an optional performance choice, not a condition for NAT reachability. If added, bound held demands and work per driver step. [revision:68](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:68), [original design:131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:131), [original design:194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:194)\n    18\t- **Q3 — retain the earlier consensus, clarified:** the peer’s **own composition** mints its opaque random ID; no meeting peer assigns it. A public-key hash without proof of control provides no authenticated peer binding. Discovery’s kickoff-hash rule concerns stream identity and verifies signatures; it is not an existing peer-registration rule. Define duplicate `:meet/here` handling in the meeting convention. [revision:69](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69), [dao.stream.discovery.md:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:70), [dao.stream.md:1087](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:1087)\n    19\t- **Q4 — agree that policy moves to M’s composition; retain the earlier policy’s full scope.** Bound request admission and work, announcements, inbox count and bytes, and idle lifetime. Open admission belongs to explicitly trusted deployments; postage remains a possible public policy. Calling M an ordinary peer does not remove the resources it allocates. [revision:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:70), [dao.stream.discovery.md:159](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:159)\n    20\t\n    21\t**Q1, Q5, and Q7 keep their decisions**, but Q1 must correlate both hops’ append outcomes, Q5 must distinguish an outer inbox gap from a final source gap, and Q7 needs the wider prose correction. **Q6 is operationally affected:** a source’s served-entry lifetime is insufficient if M retires the inbox pair while a migrated task still needs that route. [revision:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55), [revision:158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:158)\n    22\t\n    23\t## Replacement wording for the revision\n    24\t\n    25\t- **§6.2 identity:** “A WebSocket descriptor’s `:dao.stream/identity` names the logical stream exposed at that endpoint. The acceptor’s peer ID is separate reachability data under `:serve/peer`; dialing or accepting confers no stream ownership.” A peer ID cannot silently replace the contract’s logical-stream identity. [revision:127](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:127), [dao.stream.md:263](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:263)\n    26\t- **§7.3 meeting streams:** “M owns the request and announcement media. It serves restricted writer-only and reader-only handles respectively, retaining its own complementary handles. A meeting request has a correlation ID. M admits it under a bounded policy, records its outcome as an announcement, and attributes an observed UDP address only through an explicit association between the request and the UDP channel event; a bare peer-ID claim supplies no address or authority.” [revision:145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:145), [dao.stream.md:389](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:389)\n    27\t- **§6.4 and §7.4:** “An outer inbox gap is channel loss. Advance its outer cursor to the returned recovery cursor and recover outstanding inner requests under their existing IDs; never report that gap as eviction from the final served stream. A punch candidate reuses the UDP socket whose reflexive address M observed. It becomes direct only after a matching nonce-bearing pong confirms the path; otherwise the bounded attempt fails and the inbox path remains available.” [revision:131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:131), [revision:160](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:160)\n    28\t- **§13 criterion 10:** “The same serve and proxy implementation supports every peer; a peer may additionally choose the meeting interpreter and its explicit resource policy. Tests cover two dial-only peers, loss and reordering at both framing layers, bounded meeting work, announcement gaps, and fallback.” This matches the owner’s allowance for a convention without pretending every composition has identical configuration. [revision:184](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:184)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"The cross-read changes my Q2 view: a retry cap bounds one polling session, but it cannot also keep a peer reachable indefinitely. I’m checking the request and announcement paths now, especially whether a lost `:meet/here` response can allocate a second inbox pair."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '115,185p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '115,185p'\"","aggregated_output":"   115\t\n   116\tReplace \"the server\" with \"the serving side of a session\" throughout, and open the section with: every peer has a serve table and runs `serve-step`; a peer whose table is empty answers every `:serve/open` with `:serve/disclaim` and is otherwise indistinguishable from any other.\n   117\t\n   118\t### §6.1 Channel contract (replace items 1 and 2)\n   119\t\n   120\t1. A **writer handle** whose `append!` places one value on this channel's outbound path.\n   121\t2. A **reader handle** with the contract's reader surface, positioned on this channel's inbound values. A ws or udp attachment supplies it as the medium its adapter deposits into; a proxied stream served by another peer supplies it directly, because a proxy has a reader surface; two ring buffers supply it in process.\n   122\t\n   123\tA channel declares ordering and reliability from its parts: a deposited medium is ordered and best-effort under eviction; a proxied inbox is ordered and best-effort under the inbox's retention; UDP is unordered and best-effort.\n   124\t\n   125\t### §6.2 WebSocket channel (replace the Direction paragraph and host line)\n   126\t\n   127\t**Direction is establishment, not authority.** A WebSocket has a dialer and an acceptor because TCP does; a browser can only dial. The channel descriptor's host, port and path are the acceptor's, and its `:dao.stream/identity` is the acceptor's peer id. Once established, the channel is attached to both steps at both ends and nothing in this layer remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs node and cljd may accept or dial; cljs browser may dial. `dao.stream.serve.ws` wires an attachment, from either side, to `serve-step` and `proxy-step`.\n   128\t\n   129\t### §6.4 A channel over served streams (replaces \"A channel over a channel\")\n   130\t\n   131\tTwo proxied streams are a channel: peer A's writer is a proxy to a stream B reads, and A's reader is a proxy to a stream B writes. When a third peer M serves both streams, A and B have a channel without reaching each other, and M runs nothing but the ordinary serve-step over two ring buffers. Serve frames inside such a channel are values in M's streams and M never interprets them. This is streams as values sent through streams, and it is the whole of relaying.\n   132\t\n   133\t### §7 Reachability (replace §7.1 through §7.4)\n   134\t\n   135\t**7.1 The serve descriptor.** As before, with `:serve/peer` the holding peer's self-minted id, and `:serve/candidates` an ordered vector where each element is one of:\n   136\t\n   137\t- a **channel descriptor** (ws or udp) naming an acceptor address of the holding peer;\n   138\t- a **serve descriptor** for a stream some peer M serves that is the holding peer's inbox, together with the announcement-stream descriptor where M publishes the matching outbox: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/serve :dao.stream/identity <inbox> :serve/peer M :serve/meet <M's announcement-stream descriptor> :serve/candidates [M's channels]}`;\n   139\t- a **meeting resolution** `{:serve/meet <descriptor> :serve/peer <holding-peer-id>}`, meaning: attach to that announcement stream, append `:meet/call` to its request stream, read the holding peer's reflexive address, and attach a UDP channel to it while the holding peer does the same toward mine.\n   140\t\n   141\tThe proxy tries candidates in order; each attempt's resolution is deposited on the proxy's event medium. The descriptor carries no authorization.\n   142\t\n   143\t**7.2 Direct.** A peer with an acceptor channel, a listening ws endpoint or a bound UDP socket, is reached by a channel descriptor. This is establishment: the acceptor holds no authority the dialer lacks.\n   144\t\n   145\t**7.3 The meeting convention.** Any peer M may serve a **request stream** (writer surface; M's request medium, many writers) and an **announcement stream** (reader surface; single writer, M). An interpreter of M's composition reads the request medium and appends to the announcement stream. The vocabulary is a convention, not protocol:\n   146\t\n   147\t```clojure\n   148\t;; requests, appended by any peer through an ordinary session\n   149\t{:meet/here <peer-id>}                                   ; make me reachable through you\n   150\t{:meet/call <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}              ; I want a channel to that peer\n   151\t\n   152\t;; facts, appended by M alone\n   153\t{:meet/inbox <peer-id> :serve/in <serve descriptor> :serve/out <serve descriptor>}\n   154\t{:meet/seen  <peer-id> :meet/reflexive {:host … :port …}}   ; from :udp/attachment on the request\n   155\t{:meet/call  <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}                ; the call, republished\n   156\t```\n   157\t\n   158\tOn `:meet/here`, M creates two ring buffers, adds them to its serve table, and announces their descriptors; the named peer reads `in` and any peer may write it; the reverse for `out`. The pair, proxied, is a channel (§6.4). Retention, the number of pairs M keeps, and when an idle pair is unserved are M's composition policy, the same policy any served writer surface has. M is not a relay; it is a peer serving ring buffers, and a peer that reads M's announcements decides whether to trust them exactly as it decides whose directory to fold in `dao.stream.discovery`.\n   159\t\n   160\t**7.4 Punching.** After a call is announced, each party has the other's reflexive address from `:meet/seen`. Each `attach!`es a UDP channel to it and sends `:serve/ping` on the schedule its driver's `now` allows; the first inbound datagram from that address is deposited with that attachment identity, the composition matches it to the pending candidate, and a direct channel exists. No pong within the punch budget means the next candidate, normally the inbox pair on the same M. A symmetric NAT defeats punching and takes that path; the protocol reports a failed candidate, not a failure.\n   161\t\n   162\t**7.5 P2P symmetry** and **7.6 Liveness** unchanged, except that `:serve/ping` is also the punch probe.\n   163\t\n   164\t### §8 Host isolation (replace three rows)\n   165\t\n   166\t| Piece | clj | cljs browser | cljs node | cljd |\n   167\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   168\t| serve frames, proxy-step, serve-step, meeting interpreter | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n   169\t| ws channel, dial | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n   170\t| ws channel, accept | ✓ | — | ✓ | ✓ |\n   171\t| channel over served streams (§6.4) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n   172\t\n   173\tA browser peer is a full peer: it serves and attaches over any channel it can dial, and is reachable by any peer through an inbox pair on a peer it has dialed.\n   174\t\n   175\t### §12 Deferred (replace the authorization item)\n   176\t\n   177\tGating of a public meeting request stream (postage or capability) is an interpreter rule on M's own medium; the contract gates nothing and this layer adds nothing.\n   178\t\n   179\t### §13 Completion criteria (replace items 5 and 6)\n   180\t\n   181\t5. **Reachability without acceptance.** A peer that only dials serves a stream to a peer that only dials, through an inbox pair on a third peer that runs nothing but `serve-step` and the meeting interpreter; the third peer's serve table holds two ring buffers and no other state about the session.\n   182\t6. **Punch and fallback.** Two UDP peers behind a NAT simulator obtain a direct channel through a meeting stream's `:meet/seen` facts and `:serve/ping`; under a symmetric-NAT simulator they fall to the inbox pair, reported as a failed candidate on the proxy's event medium.\n   183\t\n   184\tAdd: 10. **Symmetry proof.** The same peer composition, with no configuration difference beyond which channels it has, plays the accepted-side, dialed-side, inbox-holding and inbox-using positions in items 5 and 6.\n   185\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
