{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Granularity|invalid-value|value.*size|size.*value|unbounded|bounded' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -60\"","aggregated_output":"78:- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n448:impossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n566:| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n658:them through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n669:absence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n671:deliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n674:When bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n692:the transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n732:logically unbounded log, however it grows or spills beneath — may exclude\n733:`full`. Logically unbounded is not physically infinite, and the line runs\n954:contract assumes storage is either bounded by eviction or kept forever.\n963:compaction, the ordinary way a replayed log stays bounded; erasure that law or\n1096:- **Granularity.** An element is a whole value; there is no byte offset within\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:13:01 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:13:01 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Q2 — CHANGE to GLM’s position.** Held reads should arrive with the meeting-convention milestone. I withdraw my claim that the retry budget adequately bounds polling: `:max-attempts` ends a session, while continued reachability through M requires the peer to start polling again. An interval bounds the *rate*, but trades it for inbound-call latency. A held demand reduces wire polling, though it still needs expiry and renewal; bound it to one demand per session, a hold duration, and finite work per M step. This need not gate serve-core loopback or direct channels. [Fable revision:61,68](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:61), [original design:129-131,194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:129).\n\n**Q3 — AGREE in substance.** “Composition-assigned” can mean the peer’s *own* composition; no meeting peer assigns its ID. One sentence resolves the format issue: **“The peer mints an opaque, unauthenticated ID with at least 128 random bits; a public-key hash is the intended self-certifying form, but it authenticates no peer until proof of key control and channel binding are specified.”** Discovery’s verified kickoff hash is a rule for *stream* identity, not an existing peer-registration rule; DaoStream currently treats identity as opaque and unauthenticated. [Fable revision:69](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69), [discovery:70-75](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:70), [contract:1087-1092](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:1087).\n\n**GLM’s additional defects**\n\n- **Public address exposure — TRUE; proposed inbox remedy only PARTLY works.** `:meet/seen` publishes the reflexive address on a shared reader stream, whereas the original introduction addressed two peers. Moving it to a named peer’s inbox does not make it private: the design publicly announces inbox descriptors and the contract supplies no authorization. State the disclosure explicitly, or specify authenticated access or encryption before claiming targeted delivery is private. [revision:47,51-53,153-158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:47), [original:232](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:232), [contract:285-291](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:285).\n- **Non-idempotent `:meet/here` — TRUE; rereading alone is insufficient.** The draft allocates a pair for every `:meet/here`; an unanswered append may already have taken effect. A cursor minted before the append helps find a published result, but absence after a reread cannot prove the request was not processed or that its announcement was not evicted. Give the request a stable ID; M deduplicates that ID and defines one active pair per peer ID, replaying the same descriptors for duplicates. [revision:149,153,158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:149), [original:113-115](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113), [contract:496-499,674-679](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:496).\n\nGLM’s proposed attribution sentence also needs correction: the `:meet/here` **value** contains a peer ID but no serve-session or attachment ID. Attachment correlation reaches the composition; it is not automatically inserted into an appended stream value. The spec must define that bridge. [revision:149-155](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:149), [contract:272-284](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:272).\n\n**Merged required spec edits**\n\n| Section | Edit | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| §3.1, §7.3 | Use the Q3 ID sentence above; define duplicate peer-ID and stable request-ID rules. | Self-minting does not authenticate a claimant or make `:meet/here` idempotent. [revision:69,149-158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69) |\n| §3.7, §7.3 | Include bounded, cancellable held reads with the meeting milestone; reissue after session loss. | Indefinite inbox reachability otherwise incurs standing wire polls. [original:129-131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:129) |\n| §6.1, §7.3 | Specify restricted visitor handles and M’s complementary handles; explicitly associate an appended request with the observed UDP attachment. | Surface restriction is allowed, but the draft neither constructs those handles nor carries request provenance. [contract:272-284,387-400](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:272), [revision:145-155](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:145) |\n| §6.2, §7.1 | Keep `:dao.stream/identity` as the logical-stream ID, separate from `:serve/peer`; provide both meeting-stream descriptors and both inbox-pair descriptors, with bounded candidate recursion. | The current draft uses the acceptor’s peer ID as stream identity and refers to a request stream without supplying its descriptor. [contract:263-270](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:263), [revision:127,135-141](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:127) |\n| §6.3, §7.4, criterion 6 | Send probes from the socket M observed, support an explicit destination on that socket, and require a matching nonce-bearing pong; test restricted-cone NAT. | A fresh `attach!` binds another socket, and a first inbound datagram alone does not establish the intended bidirectional path. [original:192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:192), [revision:160,182](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:160) |\n| §6.4 | Treat outer inbox `gap` as lost channel frames; recover outstanding inner requests without reporting final-stream eviction, and define a size profile or channel fragmentation for double-framed values. | Inner read retry cannot turn an outer gap into a source gap; two frames consume the UDP budget. [revision:55,131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55), [original:113-121,193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113) |\n| §7.3, §9, §13 | Bound request work, inbox count/bytes/lifetime, and announcement retention/fanout; define observable refusal, lagging-reader gap recovery, address-disclosure policy, and route lifetime for migration. Test loss and reordering at both framing layers. | Inbox caps alone do not bound M’s work or preserve a call announcement or migrated route. [revision:49-53,158,181-184](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:49), [contract:674-679](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:674) |\n\nCONSENSUS: Q2, Q3; OPEN: defect list (GLM has not reviewed the corrected provenance, privacy, and idempotence wording)"}}
